description:
Design a module that drives an LED based on pulse-width modulation (PWM) signals. The module takes multiple PWM inputs and controls the LED's brightness by toggling it on or off depending on the PWM duty cycle. A counter is used to create a timing reference, updating the PWM duty cycle at each overflow. When the counter reaches the duty cycle value, the LED is turned on; otherwise, it remains off. This allows for fine control over the LED's illumination, effectively generating a variable brightness effect based on the input PWM signals.
module top_module(
  input [n-1:0] pwm,
  input clk,          // Added clk input
  output led
);

code:
module top_module(
  input [n-1:0] pwm,
  input clk,          // Added clk input
  output led
);parameter n = 1; // number of PWM signals
parameter f = 100; // frequency of the PWM signals in Hz

reg [7:0] counter = 0;
reg [n-1:0] pwm_duty_cycle = 0;
reg led_state = 0;

always @(posedge clk) begin
  counter <= counter + 1;

  if (counter == 0) begin
    pwm_duty_cycle <= pwm;
  end

  if (counter == pwm_duty_cycle) begin
    led_state <= 1;
  end else begin
    led_state <= 0;
  end
end

assign led = led_state;

endmodule

